A||N||6||Elvira||Roof repair and adding hoodscoops||||5||
Z||000000||Elvira||06-29-2001||02:27 PM||||I have a couple of questions.  I have an 86GT 5speed.  The roof area around my sunroof is "chipping" off.  Does anyone know the best way to go about repairing this?  Also I am putting hoodscoops on my car and need to find out the best way to bond them to the hood.  The scoops I am putting on are like the ones on Phil Randolph's yellow coupe.  Any help would be appreciated as I am trying to do the work myself and want to make sure I do it right the first time before I paint it (doing that myself also).  Thanks in advance!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Elvira<P>Black 86GT 5sp x 2<BR>86SC t-top 58k miles||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||02:35 PM||||I guess it makes a difference if its the clear coat or the color paint that is chipping off.<P>  there were come great thread on painting with spray cans: click onthe search function above and search on 'rattle can' and they should show up. those threads will get you most of what you need to know.<P> Im planning on doing my front hood with cans as soon as I can get the car back in my garage.<P>  also search on 'targa paint' if you have a sunroof then painting the roof black looks pretty cool.<P>  Edit Note: the rattle can threads were mostly on how to prep the car before you spray it - so it applies to spray can or air gun paint jobs - its the prep that gives 90% of the final result.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Elvira||06-29-2001||08:51 PM||||It is actually the paint itself chipping down to a fiberous material (looks like fiberglass) so I have to find something like bondo to use on it first.  I don't know whether or not bondo would be usable on the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-30-2001||12:29 AM||||looks like fiberglass?!<P> sounds like maybe someone had some damage there and didnt repair it well. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||06-30-2001||07:12 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Elvira:<BR><B>It is actually the paint itself chipping down to a fiberous material (looks like fiberglass) so I have to find something like bondo to use on it first.  I don't know whether or not bondo would be usable on the car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Elvira!<BR>When I had my 84 repainted, the paint stripped off my roof real easy and it looked real bad...<BR>But it repainted real nice! Couldn't tell the roof is fiberglass...<BR>Just strip the paint,sand it down, and primer it, and you should have a smooth surface to paint...<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 06-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Elvira||06-30-2001||09:11 AM||||My husband says it is the gelcoat that is coming off.  It is chipping off in pieces about the size of a dime at a time.  I don't know if there may be some kind of bonding agent that I can put under the edges to stop it from flaking off so I can fix the areas before I sand and prep for painting.  The way it is, sanding won't take it down to a smooth surface for painting.<P>------------------<BR>Elvira<P>Black 86GT 5sp x 2<BR>86SC t-top 58k miles||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||06-30-2001||09:30 AM||||It's not really true fiberglas but rather a reinforced plastic. Any of the body fillers(I destest Bondo brand) will work just fine.||||reg||1
